<div class="section-heading">Master Entity Relationship Directory (1:N Cardinality Structure)</div>
<p style="font-size: 8pt; margin-bottom: 6pt;">
  The schema architecture below reflects the exact relationships and cardinality rules established in the Entity Relationship Diagram. All one-to-many connections ensure relational consistency, zero orphaned records, and strict referential integrity.
</p>

<table class="dir-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Parent Table (1)</th>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Child Table (0..*)</th>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Foreign Key Connector</th>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Business Logic & Integrity Rule</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_user</b> (PK: <code>user_id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_admin</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_admin.user_id</code> &rarr; <code>tbl_user.user_id</code></td>
      <td>One user profile can be designated as system administrative staff with elevated management privileges (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_user</b> (PK: <code>user_id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_guest_reservation_requests</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_guest_reservation_requests.user_id</code> &rarr; <code>tbl_user.user_id</code></td>
      <td>A registered client or applicant can initiate multiple guest plot reservation requests across cemetery sections (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_admin</b> (PK: <code>admin_id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_guest_reservation_requests</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_guest_reservation_requests.approved_by</code> / <code>rejected_by</code></td>
      <td>An authorized cemetery administrator reviews, approves, or rejects submitted guest reservation requests (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_guest_reservation_requests</b> (PK: <code>id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_bookings</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_bookings.id</code> (converts from reservation)</td>
      <td>An approved reservation request translates into a confirmed cemetery booking contract with assigned grave plot (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_bookings</b> (PK: <code>id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_booking_payment_schedule</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_booking_payment_schedule.booking_id</code> &rarr; <code>tbl_bookings.id</code></td>
      <td>Each booking contract establishes an installment amortization schedule spanning multiple due dates and receipts (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_plots</b> (PK: <code>id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_plot_pricing</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_plot_pricing.plot_id</code> &rarr; <code>tbl_plots.id</code></td>
      <td>A cemetery plot maintains structured pricing tiers and payment terms depending on lot type and section location (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_plots</b> (PK: <code>id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_plot_deceased_mapping</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_plot_deceased_mapping.plot_id</code> &rarr; <code>tbl_plots.id</code></td>
      <td>A designated physical plot can accommodate one or more deceased interments over time (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><b>tbl_deceased</b> (PK: <code>id</code>)</td>
      <td><b>tbl_plot_deceased_mapping</b></td>
      <td><code>tbl_plot_deceased_mapping.id</code> (maps deceased record)</td>
      <td>A registered deceased profile is mapped to its specific burial plot location and reservation date via the mapping entity (1:0..*).</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="section-heading">Database Normalization Analysis (1NF, 2NF, 3NF)</div>

<div class="norm-box">
  <b>1. First Normal Form (1NF) Compliance:</b>
  <ul style="margin: 3pt 0 3pt 16pt; padding: 0;">
    <li><b>Atomicity of Columns:</b> Every field contains atomic, indivisible values (e.g., separating <code>first_name</code>, <code>middle_name</code>, <code>last_name</code>, and <code>suffix</code> in <code>tbl_user</code>; separate coordinates <code>plot_x</code> and <code>plot_y</code> in <code>tbl_plots</code>).</li>
    <li><b>Elimination of Repeating Groups:</b> Payment installments are not stored as comma-delimited columns in <code>tbl_bookings</code>; rather, they are factored out into <code>tbl_booking_payment_schedule</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Primary Key Defined:</b> Every table possesses an unambiguous Primary Key (<code>user_id</code>, <code>admin_id</code>, or integer <code>id</code>).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="norm-box">
  <b>2. Second Normal Form (2NF) Compliance:</b>
  <ul style="margin: 3pt 0 3pt 16pt; padding: 0;">
    <li><b>Removal of Partial Dependencies:</b> Tables with single-column primary keys automatically satisfy 2NF because no attribute can depend on only a subset of the candidate key.</li>
    <li><b>Decoupled Intersection Entities:</b> Intersection mappings like <code>tbl_plot_deceased_mapping</code> use a surrogate Primary Key (<code>id</code>) while maintaining Foreign Keys to <code>plot_id</code> and <code>user_id</code>, ensuring each non-key attribute depends entirely on the whole key.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="norm-box">
  <b>3. Third Normal Form (3NF) Compliance:</b>
  <ul style="margin: 3pt 0 3pt 16pt; padding: 0;">
    <li><b>Elimination of Transitive Dependencies:</b> Non-primary key attributes do not depend on other non-primary key attributes.</li>
    <li><b>Pricing Isolation:</b> Plot pricing terms are isolated in <code>tbl_plot_pricing</code> rather than hardcoded inside <code>tbl_plots</code>, ensuring modifications to monetary terms do not redundantly alter spatial coordinates.</li>
    <li><b>Deceased Profiling:</b> Detailed biographical records of the deceased reside independently in <code>tbl_deceased</code>, removing redundant duplication across multiple reservation attempts or transfers.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

